-- title:
--   Hecke generator at p preserves vanishing étale component
-- statement:
--   The setting is the two‑chart model of $X_1(Mp)$ over a discrete valuation ring. Fix a prime $p$ and an integer $M \neq 0$ with $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$. Let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero that is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of type $\{p\}$, and let $\zeta \in L$ be a primitive $p$-th root of unity. Let $K$ be an intermediate field of $L \subseteq L((q))$ with `hK` asserting $K =$ [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), the $L$-subfield of $L((q))$ generated by the coefficientwise image of the intermediate field [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L137) of $\mathbb{Q} \subseteq \mathbb{Q}((q))$. Let $A$ be a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$, such that $p$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$ (`hAp`) and $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A$ in $L$ (`hζA`), with $K$ an $A$-algebra compatibly with $A \subseteq L \subseteq K$. Let $j \in K$ be a nonzero element whose Laurent series is the coefficientwise image of [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) (`hj`), the $q$-expansion of the modular function $j$. The curve over $A$ is the structure morphism [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L252) to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ of the two-chart model, obtained by gluing the spectra of the finite and infinite chart algebras of $K$ attached to $j$; it is assumed proper.
--
--   Special fibre and its two components. Let $k$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$ and an $A$-algebra, and let $c_1 : C_1 \to \operatorname{Spec} k$, $c_2 : C_2 \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ be proper, smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral. Let $i_1, i_2$ be closed immersions of $C_1$, $C_2$ into the base change of the model to $k$, each commuting with the structure morphisms. The hypothesis `hcover` says that every point of that base change lies in the image of $i_1$ or of $i_2$; `hred` says the fibre product of $i_1$ and $i_2$ is reduced; $n$ is a natural number with `hn` asserting that this fibre product has exactly $n$ points and `hn0` that $n > 0$. Sections: $\varepsilon$ is a section of the model over $\operatorname{Spec} A$, $\varepsilon_1$, $\varepsilon_2$ are $k$-points of $c_1$, $c_2$, and `hε₁` says that $\varepsilon_1$ followed by $i_1$ is the base change of $\varepsilon$ to $k$.
--
--   Relative $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ data. $D$ is a `RelativePic0Designation` for the model: a scheme over $\operatorname{Spec} A$ with a zero section. The hypothesis `hrep` provides (nonemptily) the datum `RepresentsRelSubPic` for the model, the section $\varepsilon$ and the cut `algEquivZeroCut` — that is, a Poincaré rigidified line bundle on $D$ that is fibrewise algebraically equivalent to zero, universal among such rigidified line bundles, and trivial along the zero section — while `hsm` and `hsep` require $D \to \operatorname{Spec} A$ to be smooth and separated. Similarly `hreps` is such a representability datum for the base change of the model to $k$ with the base-changed section and the base change $D \times_A k$, and `hPk` asserts that its Poincaré bundle is isomorphic to the base change to $k$ of the Poincaré bundle of `hrep`. $D_1$ and $D_2$ are designations for $c_1$, $c_2$ with representability data `hrep₁`, `hrep₂`. The morphism $\nu_2$ goes from $D \times_A k$ to $D_2$ over $\operatorname{Spec} k$, and `hν₂` characterises it: for every $k$-scheme $t : T \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ and every $T$-point $a$ of $D \times_A k$, the bundle classified by $a$ followed by $\nu_2$ is isomorphic to the rigidification along $\varepsilon_2$ (in the sense of `Scheme.Modules.rigidify`) of the pullback, along the morphism $C_2 \times_k T \to X_k \times_k T$ induced by $i_2$, of the bundle classified by $a$.
--
--   Generic fibre over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$. With $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ` an $A$- and $L$-algebra compatibly, $M\eta$ is a `CurveModel` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ for the function field [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182) (a smooth proper integral curve together with an isomorphism of its function field with that field and a bijection of its closed points with the places), $e\eta$ is an isomorphism of $M\eta.C$ with the base change of the model to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ commuting with the structure morphisms (`heη`). The chart open set pulled back along $e\eta$ followed by the first projection is nonempty, and `hMηpin` pins the $q$-expansions: for every element $a$ of the finite chart algebra, the function-field element obtained from the germ at the generic point of the pullback of $a$ equals the coefficientwise image under $L \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the Laurent series of $a$. The hypothesis `hgal` is Galois equivariance of the dictionary between $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points and places: for every $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra automorphism $g$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ fixing the image of $L$ pointwise and all $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x, x'$ of $M\eta.C$, if $x'$ followed by $e\eta$ and the first projection equals $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $g$ followed by the corresponding composite for $x$, then the place of $x'$ is the image of the place of $x$ under [`ModularCurve.arithmeticGalois`](def/ModularCurve_ArithmeticGalois.html#L54) applied to $g$.
--
--   The special-fibre group. $G$ is a [`ModularCurve.JOneP.NeronSpecialFibreGeom p`](def/ModularCurve_JOnePGeom.html#L9): abelian groups $G.J0s$, $G.JI$, $G.JE$, a subgroup $G.torus \le G.J0s$, and a surjective homomorphism $G.proj : G.J0s \to G.JI \times G.JE$ with kernel $G.torus$. The bijections `pts`, `ptsI`, `ptsE` identify $G.J0s$, $G.JI$, $G.JE$ with the $k$-points of $D \times_A k$, $D_1$, $D_2$ respectively; `hadd`, `haddI`, `haddE` state that each of these bijections is additive in the sense that the Poincaré bundle pulled back along the point attached to $a + b$ is isomorphic to the tensor product of those attached to $a$ and $b$; and `hproj` states, for every $x \in G.J0s$, that `ptsI` of the first component of $G.proj\,x$ is $\mathrm{pts}\,x$ followed by the classifying morphism `RepresentsRelSubPic.pullbackHom` associated with $i_1$, and that `ptsE` of the second component of $G.proj\,x$ is $\mathrm{pts}\,x$ followed by $\nu_2$.
--
--   Generic points and Hecke action. `gpts` is a bijection between [`ModularCurve.JOne (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L186) — the degree-zero divisor class group of [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182) over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ — and the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of $D$, with `hgadd` asserting that it is additive for the relative group law supplied by `hrep`. The map $\varphi$ sends each element of [`ModularCurve.HeckeAlgOne`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L16) (the polynomial ring $\mathbb{Z}[X_{\ell}, X_n]$ indexed by primes and naturals) to an endomorphism of $D$ over $\operatorname{Spec} A$; `hφmul` says each $\varphi(t)$ is a homomorphism for the relative group law at every base, and `hφpts` says that, for the module structure [`ModularCurve.heckeModuleOneBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L129), the point attached to $t \cdot x$ is the point attached to $x$ followed by $\varphi(t)$.
--
--   Abel–Jacobi data. `hDL` is a representability datum for the base change of the model to $L$ with the base-changed section and $D \times_A L$; $ajL$ is a morphism from the curve over $L$ to $D \times_A L$ over $\operatorname{Spec} L$; $kL$ is a morphism from the base change of the model to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ to its base change to $L$; $ajbar$ is a morphism $M\eta.C \to D.P$; and $\bar\varepsilon$ is a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point of $M\eta.C$. The compatibilities are: `hPL` (the Poincaré bundle over $L$ is the base change of that of `hrep`); `hajLε` ($\varepsilon_L$ followed by $ajL$ is the zero section); `hajL` (for every field $K'$, every $t : \operatorname{Spec} K' \to \operatorname{Spec} L$ and every $K'$-point $x$ of the curve over $L$, the Poincaré bundle pulled back along $x$ followed by $ajL$ is isomorphic to the line bundle of the degree-one relative Cartier divisor cut out by $x$ tensored with the ideal module of the divisor cut out by the section, so that $ajL$ realises $x \mapsto [x] - [\varepsilon]$); `hkL₁`, `hkL₂` (compatibility of $kL$ with both projections); `hajbar` ($ajbar$ is $e\eta$ followed by $kL$, $ajL$ and the first projection of $D \times_A L$); `hajbar_over` ($ajbar$ followed by $D \to \operatorname{Spec} A$ equals $M\eta \to \operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb{Q}} \to \operatorname{Spec} A$); `hεbar` ($\bar\varepsilon$ maps to the base change of $\varepsilon$); `hεbar_aj` ($\bar\varepsilon$ followed by $ajbar$ is the base change of the zero section); and `hpts_aj` (for all $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x, s$ of $M\eta.C$ with $s$ lying over $\varepsilon$, there is a degree-zero divisor $Dv$ equal to $(\text{place of } x) - (\text{place of } s)$ whose class satisfies $\mathrm{gpts}(\,[Dv]\,) = x$ followed by $ajbar$).
--
--   The Igusa component. $w$ is an `IntegralWeightOneForm` for $k$ and $M$: a weight-one modular form on $\Gamma_1(M)$ with an integral $q$-expansion whose reduction over $k$ is nonzero. $Mdl_1$ is a curve model over $k$ for the Igusa function field [`ModularCurve.igusaFunctionFieldX1C k M w`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L35), and $e_1$ an isomorphism of its underlying curve with $C_1$ over $\operatorname{Spec} k$ (`he₁`). The relevant chart open set is nonempty (`hne₁`), and `hgauss₁` is the Gauss reading of $q$-expansions on $C_1$: for every element $a$ of the finite chart algebra and all power series $x, y$ over $A$ with $y$ having nonzero reduction over $k$ and $a \cdot y = x$ as Laurent series over $L$, the function-field element of the Igusa field obtained from $a$ by the germ construction along $e_1$ followed by $i_1$ and the first projection has Laurent series the quotient of the reductions of $x$ and $y$ over $k$. The isomorphism $\theta_1$ identifies $G.JI$ with $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the Igusa function field over $k$, and `hθpin₁` pins it down on differences of points: for $g \in G.JI$ and a $k$-point $x$ of $c_1$, if the Poincaré bundle pulled back along $\mathrm{ptsI}\,g$ is isomorphic to the line bundle of the divisor of $x$ tensored with the ideal module of the divisor of $\varepsilon_1$, then there is a degree-zero divisor $Dv$ equal to $(\text{place of } x) - (\text{place of } \varepsilon_1)$, the places being taken through $e_1$, with $\theta_1 g = [Dv]$. Finally $frobIg$ is a semilinear automorphism of the Igusa function field over $k$ (a compatible pair of ring automorphisms of the field and of $k$) acting on Laurent coefficients by $p$-th power (`hfrobIg`).
--
--   The norm-free abelian subscheme. $\mathcal{A}$ is a scheme with a morphism $a$ to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ and $\iota$ a morphism $\mathcal{A} \to D.P$ over $\operatorname{Spec} A$, subject to: `h𝒜cl` ($\iota$ is a closed immersion); `h𝒜pr` and `h𝒜sm` ($a$ is proper and smooth); `h𝒜conn` (for every algebraically closed field and every morphism of its spectrum to $\operatorname{Spec} A$, the corresponding fibre of $a$ is connected); `h𝒜grp` (at every base $s$, the points of $\mathcal{A}$ map under $\iota$ onto a subgroup for the relative group law: the unit is in the image, and products and inverses of images are images); `h𝒜gen` (for $x \in$ [`ModularCurve.JOne (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L186), $x$ lies in [`ModularCurve.normFreePartAt (M * p) p`](def/ModularCurve_X1PrimitiveSpecializationAtP.html#L29) — the image of the endomorphism `normFreeEnd` — if and only if the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point $\mathrm{gpts}\,x$ of $D$ factors through $\iota$); and `h𝒜hecke` (for every $t$ and every base, the image under $\iota$ of a point of $\mathcal{A}$, followed by $\varphi(t)$, is again of that form).
--
--   Place data. $Pl$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $p$ a non-unit in it (`hPl`), $\rho : A \to Pl$ is a ring homomorphism compatible with $A \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (`hρ`), $O$ is a subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ contained in $Pl$ (`hO`) with a homomorphism $\rho_O : A \to O$ again compatible with $A \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (`hρO`), and $\pi_k : Pl \to k$ is a ring homomorphism with $A \to k$ equal to $\pi_k \circ \rho$ (`hAlgk`) and $\pi_k$ surjective (`hπk`).
--
--   Conclusion. For every $x \in$ [`ModularCurve.JOne (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L186), all $z, z'$ among the $O$-points of $D$ (morphisms over $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\rho_O$) and all $y, y' \in G.J0s$: if $x$ lies in [`ModularCurve.normFreePartAt (M * p) p`](def/ModularCurve_X1PrimitiveSpecializationAtP.html#L29); if the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point $\mathrm{gpts}\,x$ of $D$ is $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the inclusion $O \hookrightarrow \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ followed by $z$; if the $k$-point $\mathrm{pts}\,y$ of $D \times_A k$, followed by the first projection to $D.P$, equals $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\pi_k$ composed with the inclusion $O \hookrightarrow Pl$, followed by $z$; if $z'$ is $z$ followed by $\varphi$ of [`ModularCurve.heckeGenOne`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L18) at the prime $p$; if the $k$-point $\mathrm{pts}\,y'$, followed by the first projection, equals $\operatorname{Spec}$ of that same residue homomorphism followed by $z'$; and if the second component of $G.proj\,y$ is zero — then the second component of $G.proj\,y'$ is zero.
--
--   This is the statement that, on the norm-free part of $J_1(Mp)$, reduction into the kernel of the projection to the étale component of the Néron special fibre is preserved by the Hecke generator at $p$; equivalently, the matrix of the reduced $U_p$ on $G.JI \times G.JE$ is upper triangular, with vanishing corner from the Igusa (cusp) copy into the étale copy. It is the step used in establishing the ordinary filtration of $J_1(Mp)$ at $p$, and is cited by the results on the norm-free family and its pairing, among them [`ModularCurve.XOneP.normFreePartFamily_toPic0Pair_sp_heckeOperatorOneBar_snd_eq_zero_twoChartModel_x1_mul`](thm.html#ModularCurve.XOneP.normFreePartFamily_toPic0Pair_sp_heckeOperatorOneBar_snd_eq_zero_twoChartModel_x1_mul); its proof invokes the companion statement [`ModularCurve.XOneP.proj_snd_eq_zero_of_proj_snd_eq_zero_of_pts_reduction_heckeGenOne_of_surjective_residue_of_gaussReading_twoChartModel_x1_mul`](thm.html#ModularCurve.XOneP.proj_snd_eq_zero_of_proj_snd_eq_zero_of_pts_reduction_heckeGenOne_of_surjective_residue_of_gaussReading_twoChartModel_x1_mul).
